# Hostile audit — open-semicircle support for adjacent switching diameter directions

**Date:** 2026-09-15
**Reviewed:** `research/target_c_adjacent_switching_open_semicircle_2026-09-15.md`
**Disposition:** `PASS` at the stated exact structural scope.
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The candidate adds one exact geometric fact to the current adjacent-strip stack:
orient every switching actual diameter from its endpoint outside the middle layer toward
its endpoint inside the middle layer. The resulting unit directions all lie in one open
semicircle. The main downstream consequence is that the positive switching KKT
resultant cannot vanish.

The vulnerable points are the support-normal sign, the use of contact-graph boundary
arcs versus convex-hull arcs, the one-orientation case where middle endpoints may occupy
two arcs, and the upgrade from a closed to an open semicircle. All pass.

## 1. The support-normal sign is correct

For a diameter pair `xm`, put

\[
d=(m-x)/D.
\]

For every source point `z`,

\[
d\cdot(z-x)\le |z-x|\le D=d\cdot(m-x).
\]

Thus `d dot z <= d dot m`; by convexity the inequality holds on the whole source hull.
So `d` is an outward support normal at `m`. Reversing the pair gives `-d` as an outward
support normal at `x`.

There is no sign reversal here: the vector from the exterior switching endpoint into
the middle endpoint points in an outward support-normal direction at the middle
endpoint.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 2. The outer-face order is enough to use convex-hull normal order

The adjacent switching-order theorem already proves that every actual diameter endpoint
is an extreme point of the source convex hull, and that the cyclic order inherited from
the contact-graph outer face agrees with convex-hull cyclic order on those endpoints.

Therefore an outer-face block containing all relevant diameter endpoints corresponds,
after deleting non-extreme outer-face vertices, to one cyclic interval of extreme hull
vertices. The proof need not identify the entire contact-graph boundary arc with a
straight or smooth convex-hull arc.

On a convex polygon the outward Gauss map is monotone in cyclic order. Along an edge it
is constant; at a vertex its normal cone is exactly the angular interval between the
two adjacent edge normals. Hence a cyclic interval of extreme vertices has one
connected normal-angle sweep. Complementary cyclic intervals have complementary sweeps
whose angular lengths sum to `2pi`, after assigning their common endpoint cones by
one-sided convention.

No switching endpoint lies at the artificial separators between the four open boundary
blocks, so that endpoint convention cannot change membership of a switching support
normal.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 3. Mixed switch orientations give one middle arc

If both left- and right-switch rows occur, the audited one-arc theorem places every
switching middle endpoint on one common middle boundary arc `I`. Every exterior
switching endpoint lies in the complementary cyclic hull interval `J`.

Let `alpha` be the normal-turn length of `I`.

- If `alpha<=pi`, every `d_h` belongs to the normal sweep of `I`, a closed semicircle.
- If `alpha>pi`, the complementary sweep has length `2pi-alpha<pi`; every `-d_h`
  belongs to that sweep, so every `d_h` belongs to its antipodal open semicircle.

This is exact and uses no near-disk approximation.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 4. One switch orientation is not a loophole

If only left-switch rows occur, the middle endpoints may indeed occupy both middle
outer-face arcs. The candidate does not incorrectly treat their union as connected.
Instead all exterior switching endpoints lie in the single left block `E=B_L`.

The complement of that cyclic interval contains every middle switching endpoint. If the
normal sweep of `E` has length at most `pi`, all `-d_h` lie in one closed semicircle. If
it has length greater than `pi`, its complementary normal sweep has length below `pi`
and contains all `d_h`.

The all-right case is symmetric.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 5. Closed semicircle upgrades to open semicircle

A finite set contained in a closed semicircle fails to fit in an open semicircle only
when its minimal circular span is exactly `pi`, in which case the finite set contains
both antipodal endpoints of that span.

For switching directions this would give distinct edges `h_1,h_2` with

\[
d_{h_2}=-d_{h_1}.
\]

Their actual diameter segments are parallel in one projective direction. They cannot be
the same graph edge listed twice: each switching edge has a unique orientation from its
exterior endpoint into its middle endpoint. The audited parallel-diameter uniqueness
lemma says two distinct parallel diameter pairs cannot occur in a finite diameter-`D`
set.

Hence the antipodal endpoint case is impossible and the switching direction set lies
in an open semicircle.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 6. The KKT resultant consequence is strictly stronger than the current two-bar Helly theorem

Current main already has an independent one/two-bar tangent certificate, so the
candidate's reduction of the generic Caratheodory witness to at most two diameter bars
is no longer strategically new.

The open-semicircle statement adds a different exact conclusion. For the canonical
relative-interior stress,

\[
\beta_h>0
\]

on every actual switching diameter. A positive combination of vectors in one open
semicircle cannot vanish. Therefore

\[
\sum_{h\in H_{sw}}\beta_hd_h\ne0
\]

and the strip balance forces

\[
Aa+Bb\ne0.
\]

Thus the zero-resultant branch in the current three-ray/KKT-resultant classifier is
empty. In the antipodal boundary case `b=-a`, this also gives `A!=B`.

This conclusion is not supplied by the Helly tangent certificate alone.

**Verdict:** PASS and genuinely additional.

## 7. Regression check

The companion finite regression samples strictly convex ellipse polygons with random
cyclic vertex positions, computes all actual diameter pairs, and checks every cyclic
vertex interval. For every diameter edge crossing the interval cut it orients the edge
from outside to inside and tests open-semicircle containment.

The deterministic seed used by the committed script checks `278066` nonempty cut
instances and finds no counterexample.

This is only a regression; the proof is the support-normal/Gauss-map argument above.

## Final assessment

The theorem survives hostile review. Its main frontier effect should be stated as

```text
adjacent switching KKT resultant is always nonzero
+ no diameter-only three-ray positive circuit
+ current one/two-bar tangent classifier is compatible with a common open-semicircle
  support geometry.
```

C-II remains open. The next useful step is to combine the nonzero resultant and the
one-arc prefix/hub/suffix order with the remaining one/two-bar tangent certificate,
especially in the nearly antipodal boundary-contact regime forced by adjacent
phase-collapse.

**Final verdict:** PASS.
